- How would you test stack growth under multithreaded conditions?
- What changes would you make to support very large stacks or reduce guard-page fragmentation?

Why This Is Asked: tests understanding of synchronization primitives, fairness, and memory ordering. Key Concepts: ticket locks, wake queues, avoiding starvation, spurious wakeups, memory barriers. Code Example: see below. Follow-up: discuss adaptation to multicore/NUMA, impact of preemption, and testing strategies.

Code Example:
```c
#include <stdatomic.h>

typedef struct {
  atomic_uint next;
  atomic_uint nowServing;
} ticket_lock_t;

void ticket_lock_init(ticket_lock_t *lk){
  atomic_store(&lk->next, 0);
  atomic_store(&lk->nowServing, 0);
}

unsigned ticket_lock_acquire(ticket_lock_t *lk){
  unsigned ticket = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&lk->next, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  while (atomic_load_explicit(&lk->nowServing, memory_order_acquire) != ticket) {
    _mm_pause();
  }
  return ticket;
}

void ticket_lock_release(ticket_lock_t *lk){
  atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&lk->nowServing, 1, memory_order_release);
}
```

Follow-up Questions:
- How would you test fairness under heavy contention?
- How would you extend to multiple locks and prevent deadlock?

## Why This Is Asked

Signals understanding of fundamental preemption, context switching, and simple scheduling logic using a timer interrupt.

## Key Concepts

- Preemptive scheduling
- Timer ISR and time-slice accounting
- Context switch mechanics
- Basic synchronization around scheduler state

## Code Example

```c
// Pseudo-C like sketch
typedef struct Task { int time_slice; /* ... */ } Task;
volatile Task* current;
void timer_isr() {
  // disable_interrupts(); // optional, depending on platform
  current->time_slice--;
  if (current->time_slice <= 0) {
    schedule(); // enqueue current and pick next; performs context switch
  }
  // enable_interrupts();
}
```

