## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical skills in NUMA locality, thread affinity, and measurable performance impact.

## Key Concepts
- NUMA locality and memory policies
- Thread and data placement
- Per‑socket pools and queues
- Performance measurement with perf and hardware counters

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pin a thread to CPU set on Linux (example approach)
#include <pthread.h>
#include <sched.h>

void pin_thread(int cpu) {
  cpu_set_t cpuset; CPU_ZERO(&cpuset); CPU_SET(cpu, &cpuset);
  pthread_setaffinity_np(pthread_self(), sizeof(cpu_set_t), &cpuset);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate that per‑socket locality reduces cache misses under bursty load?
- What trade‑offs arise if a worker must be migrated for load balancing?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of timer interrupts, context switches, and simple schedulers at kernel level. It also probes how aging prevents starvation.

## Key Concepts
- Preemptive scheduling and round-robin
- Timer interrupt handling and context-switch protocol
- Per-process state and a ready-queue data structure
- Aging to prevent starvation under load

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-kernel RR tick
function timerTick(running, ready) {
  running.quantum--;
  if (running.quantum <= 0) {
    ready.enqueue(running);
    const next = ready.dequeue();
    contextSwitch(running, next);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to implement tickless mode with RR?
- How would you test aging efficacy under bursty workloads?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical I/O scheduling under virtualization and fairness.

## Key Concepts
- Per-VM token bucket
- I/O throttling and burst handling
- Deferred queues and deadlines
- Fairness and starvation prevention

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct {
  int tokens;
  int rate; // tokens/sec
  int bucket;
  spinlock_t lock;
} vm_throttle_t;

void on_io_arrival(vm_throttle_t* t) {
  acquire(&t->lock);
  if (t->tokens > 0) { t->tokens--; enqueue_io(); }
  else { defer_io(); }
  release(&t->lock);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to adjust rates dynamically under load?
- How to extend to multiple I/O queues (separate reads/writes)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of demand paging, synchronization, and TLB consistency in a realistic but simple setup.

## Key Concepts
- Demand paging and lazy page-in
- Per-page locking to serialize concurrent faults
- Correct handling of IO vs zero-fill paths
- Page-table updates and TLB invalidation
- Wakeup semantics for waiters

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal fault handler sketch (C-like)
void handle_fault(Page* p){
  acquire(p->lock);
  if (p->present) { release(p->lock); return; }
  if (p->loading) { wait_on(p); release(p->lock); /* queue waiter */ return; }
  p->loading = true; release(p->lock);
  // IO or zero-fill
  if (has_backing_file(p)) read_file_into(p);
  else zero_fill(p);
  acquire(p->lock);
  p->loading = false; p->present = true; update_pte(p);
  flush_tlb(p);
  wake_waiters(p);
  release(p->lock);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test race conditions with two faulting threads? 
- How would you handle I/O errors from the backing file? 
- How would this scale to many pages in parallel?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical virtual memory implementation skills including COW semantics, page table management, reference counting, synchronization, and TLB consistency. It evaluates the ability to handle concurrent page faults without memory leaks or race conditions.

## Key Concepts
- Copy-On-Write (COW) optimization
- Page table updates and TLB invalidation
- Race condition handling and synchronization primitives
- Concurrent memory management

## Code Example
```c
void handle_write_fault(PTE *pte) {
  acquire(pte->lock);
  if (pte->refcount > 1) {
    Page *newpage = alloc_page();
    memcpy(newpage->addr, pte->frame->addr, PAGE_SIZE);
    pte->frame = newpage;
    pte->writable = 1;
    pte->refcount--;
    invalidate_tlb(pte->vaddr);
  } else {
    pte->writable = 1;
  }
  release(pte->lock);
}
```
